## What I did

src/dispatch_sub.rs had one test bracketing itself with set_current_dir(tmp) … set_current_dir(prev). That call is process-global, so it corrupted any test resolving a relative path at that moment — the live setup::tests flake in the trajectory. Those tests are victims, not culprits; #[serial] never protected them because it only serialises against other #[serial] tests.

The seam, and why not a dir-taking one. The task suggested thing_in(root, …). I didn't do that, and the reason is the route: this test drives try_dispatch_subcommand → handle_goal → save_goal → goal_path → goal_base end-to-end, so a root parameter would have had to thread through a 36-arm dispatcher — two risks in a revert-all-or-nothing task. commands_goal.rs already owned the right seam: a #[cfg(test)] thread-local `GOAL_BASE_DIR that *every* goal path resolves through. I made it reachable from a sibling module as pub(crate) fn with_goal_base_dir(dir, f)` (restores the **previous** value, not `None`, so nesting is safe; restores on panic), and pointed the module's own private with_temp_dir at it so the rule has one statement instead of two copies. A thread-local beats a chdir on the property that matters here: each test thread gets its own, so it cannot leak.

#[serial] came off with the chdir. The file's two remaining ones are on ANTHROPIC_API_KEY env-var tests — a different process-global, still legitimately serial — so use serial_test::serial; stays.

One thing I added beyond the ask. Every assertion the test already had could have been satisfied by a regression that wrote the real repo's `.yoyo/goal.md — which is exactly the damage #780 names. So it now asserts at the emission point: tmp/.yoyo/goal.md` must exist. Verified the repo's own goal file stayed untouched through the run.

